  "query_preview": "..\nThursday: Stratus and fog is still sitting over valleys in the \nInland Northwest, and clouds are moving through ahead of a low \npressure system moving in on Friday. Upper-level ridging will keep \ntemperatures well above normal for mid-November, with highs in the \nmid to high 50s and even some 60s down in the Lewiston/Pullman \nareas. To compare, normal high temperatures are in the low to mid \n40s. Valleys still under stratus and fog could see their high \ntemperatures be a little cooler than forecast, as models typically \nstruggle with low stratus and fog.  \n\nFriday through Tuesday: Starting tomorrow (Friday), a series of low \npressure systems will move through the area. The first system will \nmove out of the area on Saturday, and the second will move in Sunday \nthrough Monday. By Saturday, temperatures will moderate slightly, \ndropping down into the low 50s, but will still be warmer than \nnormal. Precipitation amounts associated with this first system will \nbe low, with ranges from 0.05 to 0.2 inches. Highest totals will be \nseen in the mountains. Snow levels at this time look to be high, \nstarting at around 7000 feet and lowering to 5000 feet, with impacts \nlimited to elevations above mountain pass level. As is typical with \na system moving in, winds will increase across the Cascades and \nColumbia Basin Friday and Saturday night, but wind gusts of 15 to 25 \nmph will keep impacts low. \n\nSunday through Tuesday, the second of the two low pressure systems \nwill impact the Inland Northwest. This system will usher in colder \nair, with temperatures cooling to the high 40s by Monday, then \ncooling a couple degrees further by Tuesday. Snow levels will start \nout at around 5000 feet, but by Monday will drop to 4000 feet. This \nwill bring snow to mountain passes, but totals are have been \ngradually dropping through the week. Through Tuesday morning, \nWashington Pass is set to see the most snow, with a 59 percent \nchance of seeing 3 inches or more. Chances of 4 inches drop to below \n20%. For Stevens Pass, nearly no snow is expected, with less than a \n10 percent chance of 1 inch of snow.  Precipitation amounts range \nfrom 0.15 to 0.4 inches in the lowlands to up to 0.75 inches in the \nmountains. Winds in the Columbia Basin will increase Sunday, with \nwind gusts 15 to 20 mph expected. \n\nWednesday through Thursday: Model consensus drops considerably as we \nmove into the middle to end of next week. Ensemble cluster analysis \nshows a low moving through, but the track and strength of the low is \nwidely disagreed upon. with a 50/50 split on if we'll be seeing \nhigher heights or lower heights over the area. Should the low be \nfurther southward, chances for precipitation will lower; should the \nlow be further northward, chances for precipitation are higher. \nTemperatures look to be nearing right around normal (highs in the \nlow 40s, and lows in the low 30s). /AS",
